sections.s2_technical:
Let me explain ownership carefully, because getting it wrong is the single most common relationship bug I see from juniors.

A foreign key is a single column on ONE physical table. In an account-to-trades relationship, the `trades` table has an `account_id` column. There is no column on the `accounts` table pointing at trades. So in JPA terms, the child (`Trade`) is the natural OWNER of the relationship, because its table physically stores the foreign key. We express that with `@ManyToOne` and `@JoinColumn(name = "account_id")` on the `Trade` entity. `@JoinColumn` tells Hibernate the exact name of the foreign-key column to read and write.

```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "account_id")   // this Trade row's FK column
private Account account;
```

On the parent side, the account references a collection of trades with `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "account")`. The crucial word is `mappedBy`, and its value is the NAME OF THE FIELD on the child entity that owns the relationship — here the `account` field on `Trade`. It is NOT a column name. `mappedBy` marks this side as the INVERSE side, which means Hibernate will NOT generate any SQL from changes to this collection by itself; it reads the foreign key only from the owning side.

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
```

Now the subtle part that bites everyone. Because only the owning side drives the SQL, if you add a trade ONLY to the parent collection and forget to set the child's `account` field, Hibernate writes a row with a NULL `account_id`. The fix is a synchronisation helper on the parent that updates BOTH sides at once:

```java
public void addTrade(Trade trade) {
    trades.add(trade);          // inverse side (for in-memory navigation)
    trade.setAccount(this);     // owning side (this is what Hibernate actually persists)
}
public void removeTrade(Trade trade) {
    trades.remove(trade);
    trade.setAccount(null);
}
```

A unidirectional alternative exists. If the parent does not need to navigate to its children, you can put `@OneToMany @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")` on the parent WITHOUT any field on the child. But this is less efficient — Hibernate issues extra UPDATE statements to set the foreign key after inserting children, so bidirectional with `mappedBy` is the recommended production pattern.

Two more decisions that matter in production. First, fetch type: `@ManyToOne` defaults to EAGER, which I always override to `FetchType.LAZY` to avoid accidental over-fetching, while `@OneToMany` already defaults to LAZY. Second, cascade and orphan removal: `cascade = CascadeType.ALL` makes saving the parent also save its trades, and `orphanRemoval = true` means removing a trade from the parent collection issues a `DELETE` for that row. In a banking domain I use cascade carefully — it is correct for owned children like trades belonging to an account, but I would never cascade a REMOVE from an account into shared reference data.

A senior nuance on the collection type and `equals`/`hashCode`: when the children form a `Set`, I implement `equals` and `hashCode` on a stable business key (like `tradeId`) rather than the generated primary key, so an entity behaves correctly in the set both before and after it receives its database id.

